# Experimental methodology and expected results

## Proposed benchmark

Build **HARMONIC-Rescue**, an extension of the HARMONIC simulated UGV--drone lost-key search task. The UGV has RGB-D/LiDAR and a manipulation/retrieval action space; the drone has RGB camera, altitude/waypoint control, and a restricted battery. A strategic controller maintains a structured interaction graph with nodes for observations, entities, goals, plans, actions, teammate beliefs, interventions, and explanations. Edges store temporal, causal, and confidence relations. The tactical layer executes grounded navigation, search, communication, and retrieval policies. All simulator state, actions, messages, graph updates, causal parent sets, and random seeds are logged.

Use an Isaac Sim or AirSim implementation for photorealistic sensor data, with procedurally generated indoor/outdoor maps, object appearances, weather/lighting, occlusion, and obstacle layouts. Release the task generator, graph/action logs, intervention scripts, and human-readable explanations. If hardware is available, transfer a subset to a wheeled UGV and quadrotor (or drone-in-the-loop), using safety geofencing and a fixed low-level controller.

## Splits and test conditions

Create train/validation/test splits by map, object appearance, weather, task composition, and communication vocabulary--never merely by frame. Include compositional test missions that combine known subtasks in unseen orders. Evaluate four regimes: (1) nominal cooperative search, (2) exogenous disturbances (blocked corridor, moving obstacle, GPS loss, camera blur, battery drain), (3) teammate disturbances (delayed, noisy, or intentionally incorrect status messages), and (4) adversarial multimodal perturbations (localized image patches, misleading landmark descriptions, and injected but provenance-tagged messages). Hold out disturbance combinations and severity levels to test extrapolation. An oracle-state setting isolates planning from perception and a sensor-only setting isolates the full stack.

## Methods and baselines

Compare: (A) the proposed graph-based, uncertainty-aware HARMONIC controller with explicit causal event records and counterfactual explanation generation; (B) HARMONIC with an LLM/VLM planner but no structured graph; (C) a centralized multi-agent planner; (D) decentralized MAPPO or QMIX-style coordination; (E) a behavior-cloning/VLA policy; and (F) an ablated strategic layer using only reactive rules. For explanation, compare graph-derived causal summaries, post-hoc LLM rationalizations, action-log summaries, and no-explanation controls. Calibrate every model on an identical compute and communication budget.

## Protocol and metrics

Run at least 100 seeded episodes per held-out condition, with paired seeds across methods. Measure task success (keys found/retrieved, mission completion, collision and safety violations), normalized reward, time-to-completion, path length/energy, and communication bytes/turns. Measure cooperation through role allocation efficiency, redundant search fraction, information-gain per message, teammate intervention count, and recovery time after a disturbance. Report clean-versus-corrupted performance degradation, worst-group and worst-severity performance, calibration error/Brier score for predicted object identity, teammate intent, plan success, and confidence; also report abstention/selective-risk curves.

For explanations, score (i) **faithfulness** by deleting or intervening on cited causes and measuring the predicted-action change, sufficiency/comprehensiveness, and agreement with logged causal parents; (ii) **counterfactual correctness** by asking whether the explanation predicts action changes under blocked routes, altered goals, or different teammate beliefs; (iii) temporal/event accuracy against simulator logs; (iv) human usefulness and trust via blinded ratings from robotics-literate participants; and (v) explanation latency, token/message cost, and consistency across repeated runs. A crucial anti-hallucination test presents explanations to evaluators who cannot see the trace and separately evaluates whether they identify the actual causal intervention rather than merely sounding plausible.

## Ablations and analyses

Remove graph memory, causal edges, uncertainty estimates, provenance checks, counterfactual validation, teammate-belief modeling, and strategic--tactical bidirectional feedback one at a time. Evaluate combinations to expose interactions. Test calibration under shift, robustness as a function of perturbation budget, and the success--latency--explanation-fidelity Pareto frontier. Analyze failures by perception error, stale memory, incorrect teammate model, tactical execution error, communication loss, or strategic replanning error. Use bootstrap confidence intervals and paired permutation tests over seeds; report effect sizes, not only averages. Human trust analyses should control for explanation fluency so that plausible but unfaithful explanations cannot win solely by style.

## Expected results and falsifiable hypotheses

H1: The proposed controller will retain higher mission success and lower recovery time than reactive, VLA, and unstructured-LLM baselines on unseen disturbance combinations, with the largest gain when communication is delayed or misleading. H2: structured causal memory and provenance checks will improve calibration and selective risk, not just average task reward. H3: graph-derived explanations will have significantly higher intervention faithfulness, counterfactual accuracy, and event accuracy than post-hoc LLM rationalizations, at a modest latency cost. H4: removing uncertainty and causal validation may preserve nominal success but will produce overconfident failures and sharply lower worst-group performance and human trust. H5: the bidirectional strategic--tactical interface will reduce redundant exploration and improve transfer to the physical subset, while a centralized oracle may remain strongest in nominal simulation but degrade more under communication and sensor shift.

The central result should be a paired performance/explanation table and Pareto plot, demonstrating whether trustworthy explanation can be measured as causal fidelity under intervention rather than inferred from fluent text. A negative result--no fidelity or robustness gain after graph and causal modules--would still identify which HARMONIC claims do not survive controlled ablation.
